+## config-ui-fidelity-gate (skills/feature/fimplement/workflows/config-ui-fidelity-gate.js)
+
+Gate the first commit of any config-server UI change on rendered fidelity: per touched template, render it via a throwaway harness, screenshot it, diff its new inputs/selectors against the design-system stylesheet, assert exactly one Save button, and block the commit on any violation. Fronted by `/fimplement` (or `/fdesign change`) as a pre-report/pre-first-commit gate whenever a change touches config-server templates or `internal/server/assets/style.css` — invoke the skill, not the workflow, unless debugging.
+
+- **args**: `{templates, cssPath?, renderHint?}` — `templates` is the required non-empty string array of repo-relative config-server template paths the diff touches; `cssPath` is the design-system stylesheet to diff against (default `internal/server/assets/style.css`); `renderHint` is optional free-text on how the throwaway render harness is stood up. The script throws on a non-object args or an empty `templates`.
+- **What it does**: a `pipeline()` over the templates, no barrier. Render stage — one `general-purpose` agent per template stands up a **throwaway render harness** (never the live config-server, which cannot launch from a worktree because `routines.SyncAll` registers worktree paths with launchd), renders the single template with representative data, screenshots it, and returns `harness_ok`, `image_path`, `notes`. Gate stage — when the harness produced a screenshot, one agent reads the template plus `cssPath`, computes `new_selectors[]`, `unstyled_input_types[]`, and `save_button_count`, inspects the screenshot for off-spec widgets, and sets `pass` true only when there is exactly one Save button, no unstyled input, and nothing visually off-spec; a failed harness short-circuits to a `pass:false` result in pure JS (no second agent).
+- **Returns**: `{gate, results}` — `gate` is `{pass, violations}` where `pass` is true only when every template passed and `violations` is the flattened, template-prefixed list; `results` is the per-template gate object (`template`, `new_selectors`, `unstyled_input_types`, `save_button_count`, `pass`, `violations`).
+
+## subsystem-grounding (skills/feature/fdesign/workflows/subsystem-grounding.js)
+
+Ground a multi-subsystem fchange/fdesign plan against the live tree: fan one Explore agent per named subsystem, collect every agent's file:line report at a barrier, then fold each report verbatim into the plan. Fronted by `/fdesign` (or `/fdesign change`) when the drivers span two or more subsystems (e.g. UI + pipeline/schema) — invoke the skill, not the workflow, unless debugging.
+
+- **args**: `{subsystems, drivers?, planRef?}`. `subsystems` is the required non-empty string array of subsystem names the drivers touch (one Explore agent per entry); `drivers` is optional free-text passed to every agent for scoping; `planRef` is the optional repo-relative path of the plan skeleton the front drafts concurrently — when set the Fold agent folds the reports into that file in place, otherwise it returns `grounded_sections[]` for the front to fold. The script throws on a non-object args or an empty `subsystems`.
+- **What it does**: **Ground** runs one `Explore` agent per subsystem in a single `parallel()` barrier — each scopes itself to one subsystem's files in the live tree, reads the real files (never memory), and returns a `file_line_report[]` of "path:line — what is there" strings the plan consumes verbatim, plus the `worktree_ref` it read and any scope gaps. **Fold** is one agent that folds every report into the plan: with `planRef` it edits that file in place and anchors each claim to a real path:line; without one it returns one `grounded_sections[]` entry per subsystem for the front to fold. Every file:line reference is preserved verbatim — never paraphrased.
+- **Returns**: `{subsystems, reports, failed, fold}` — `reports` is one grounding object per subsystem (`subsystem`, `file_line_report`, `worktree_ref`, `notes`); `failed` lists subsystems whose Explore agent returned `null`; `fold` carries `plan_ref` (empty when no `planRef`), `grounded_sections[]`, and `notes`.
+
+## foreign-toolchain-pretag (skills/git/package-commit/workflows/foreign-toolchain-pretag.js)
+
+Gate a release tag on a clean local compile of every artifact built by a toolchain the repo doesn't run natively (Inno Setup `.iss`, GOOS cross-builds): for each artifact, build it locally — dockerized where needed — assert exit 0, and block the tag on any failure so a CI run on a pushed tag is never the first compile. Fronted by `/package-commit` (or any release-tag step) whenever the release includes a foreign-toolchain artifact — invoke the skill, not the workflow, unless debugging.
+
+- **args**: `{artifacts, tagRef?}`. `artifacts` is the required non-empty array of `{toolchain, artifact, buildCmd?, dockerized?}` objects — `toolchain` names the foreign toolchain (e.g. `inno-setup`, `goos-cross-build`), `artifact` the repo-relative source/output, `buildCmd` the local compile command (e.g. `make installer-check`, `GOOS=windows go build ...`), `dockerized` a hint that the compile runs in a container; `tagRef` is the optional tag the gate protects (reporting only — the workflow never tags). The script throws on a non-object args or an empty `artifacts`.
+- **What it does**: a `pipeline()` over the artifacts, no barrier. **Build** — one agent per artifact runs the artifact's local compile (dockerized where the toolchain isn't installed natively, e.g. `iscc` via the amake/innosetup image) from the repo root and returns `toolchain`, `artifact`, `exit_code`, and a `log_ref`. **Gate** — the script maps every non-zero (or missing) exit to a blocking error in pure JS; the gate passes only when every artifact compiled with exit 0. The gate is advisory to the front: a subagent cannot block a tag, so the front reads `gate.pass` and refuses to tag on any blocking error.
+- **Returns**: `{gate, builds}` — `gate` is `{pass, blocking_errors}` where `pass` is true only when every artifact built exit 0 and `blocking_errors` is the artifact-prefixed failure list; `builds` is the per-artifact result (`toolchain`, `artifact`, `exit_code`, `log_ref`, `notes`).
